      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "At present, an esophageal origin can be identified in as many as 50% of patients with non cardiac chest pain. In about 1/3 of these patients, gastroesophageal reflux can be documented. In the remaining 2/3 of patients, various disorders of esophageal motility have been described. Abnormal esophageal motility may be classified as nutcracker esophagus, diffuse esophageal spasm, achalasia, hypertensive lower esophageal sphincter, and nonspecific esophageal motility disorders. Simultaneous recording of intraesophageal pH, pressure, and symptoms (combined 24-h pH-metry and manometry) makes it possible to test the temporal association between pain, reflux, or abnormal motility. This review describes the diagnostic evaluation and therapeutic options in patients with non cardiac chest pain. Identification of the esophageal origin of chest pain should improve the therapeutic results."},
